NAME
    passwd - set a user's password (root only)

SYNOPSIS
    passwd [user]

DESCRIPTION
    Changes a password in /etc/passwd. With no argument, changes the current
    user's; with a name, that account's. Prompts for the new password twice (no
    echo) and stores SHA-256(salt || password) with a fresh salt.

    The salt comes from the machine's hardware RNG (a virtio-rng device, via the
    RANDOM syscall). Where there is none - which is most machines; QEMU needs
    -device virtio-rng-device, and Parallels and the Pi have none at all -
    passwd falls back to a weaker salt derived from the monotonic clock and says
    so: "no hardware RNG - using a weaker clock-derived salt".

    ROOT ONLY in this release: a non-root user changing their OWN password needs
    a privileged path (a setuid bit or an account server) that is not built yet.
    The shared account logic is in place for it, so this is a policy limit, not a
    redesign.

EXAMPLES
    # passwd user
    New password: ****
    Retype new password: ****
    passwd: password updated for user

SEE ALSO
    useradd, usermod, login, id
